          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does \"code first\" mean, and what actually goes wrong if the two are sequenced the other way round?",
          "answer": "**What \"code first\" means**\n\nIn ICD-10-CM, the system clinicians and coders use to label diagnoses, \"code first\" is an instruction about order. It says an underlying condition is recorded before its manifestation, meaning the effect or sign it produces. The \"use additional code\" note works as a partner to it. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\nBy contrast, \"code also\" means two codes are needed but does not say which goes first. (Same source.)\n\n**What goes wrong if the order is reversed**\n\nThe retrieved passages do not describe the consequences of reversing the sequence in detail. They explain the rule, not what happens when it is broken. I can't tell you from these sources what error or effect follows, so I won't guess.\n\nWhat the passages do show is that sequencing matters in ICD-10-CM. For example, in heart disease linked to high blood pressure, the combination code for hypertensive heart disease is reported first, and a heart failure code is added to specify the type. The heart failure code is added, not swapped in. (Source: *Coding heart failure: I50*.)\n\n**What the sources did not cover**\n\nThese documents do not say what a coding or record-keeping error would mean for a patient, a claim, or a statistic. A coder or the classification's official tabular list would be the right place to check the consequences of a particular sequencing error.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
